When `OrderService` creates `StripeGateway` internally with `new`, the two classes are permanently married. Every time the payment layer changes, the service that shouldn't care about it breaks.

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    // Permanently coupled to Stripe — hardcoded, unhideable
    private final StripeGateway gateway = new StripeGateway();

    public void placeOrder(Order order) {
        gateway.charge(order.getTotal());
    }
}

// Problem 1: Unit testing — StripeGateway hits the real network.
//            No seam to inject a mock. You test Stripe, not OrderService.
// Problem 2: Swap to PayPal — must modify OrderService source code.
// Problem 3: Staging env — no way to inject SandboxGateway without code change.
// Problem 4: StripeGateway changes its constructor — OrderService compile error.
```

All four problems share the same root: `OrderService` decided what it needed instead of declaring what it needs.

Replacing `new StripeGateway()` with a constructor parameter flips control. `OrderService` now declares what it needs (`PaymentGateway` — a contract), and Spring provides the matching implementation.

```java
public interface PaymentGateway {
    void charge(double amount);
}

@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final PaymentGateway gateway; // depends on the contract, not Stripe

    // Spring finds a PaymentGateway bean and passes it here
    public OrderService(PaymentGateway gateway) {
        this.gateway = gateway;
    }

    public void placeOrder(Order order) {
        gateway.charge(order.getTotal());
    }
}

// Unit test — no Spring context, no network:
var service = new OrderService(amount -> System.out.println("mock: " + amount));

// Production: @Component StripeGateway implements PaymentGateway — Spring wires it.
// Staging:    @Profile("staging") @Component SandboxGateway — Spring picks the right one.
```

`OrderService` now depends on a contract. Swap providers, write tests, configure environments — the service class never changes. That's loose coupling in practice.

The Spring team recommends constructor injection for all mandatory dependencies, and the reasoning comes down to three concrete differences that show up in real code.

**Explicit contract** — the constructor signature tells you exactly what this class needs to function. A class with six field-injected `@Autowired` dependencies looks deceptively clean from the outside. Try to test it without Spring and you discover the hidden dependencies one NPE at a time. Constructor injection makes them visible from the first line.

**Immutability** — constructor-injected fields can be `final`. Once set, they never change. Field injection makes `final` impossible: Spring writes into the field after construction using `Field.setAccessible(true)`, and the JVM's memory model doesn't guarantee visibility for reflectively-written `final` fields. Immutable beans eliminate a class of race conditions in the shared singleton instances that power every Spring application.

**Testability without the container** — a constructor-injected class can be tested with `new OrderService(mockGateway)` in a plain JUnit test. No `@SpringBootTest`, no context startup overhead, no `@MockBean` setup. Field injection requires either `ReflectionTestUtils.setField()` or a full Spring context — both slower and more fragile.

Since Spring 4.3, if a class has exactly **one constructor**, `@Autowired` is not required. Spring discovers and uses it automatically. Combined with Lombok's `@RequiredArgsConstructor` — which generates a constructor for every `private final` field — constructor injection in modern Spring requires zero boilerplate.

```java
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor  // generates constructor for all final fields
public class OrderService {
    private final PaymentGateway gateway;
    private final InventoryClient inventory;
    // No @Autowired — Spring 4.3+ uses the single constructor automatically
}
```

The most common injection **anti-pattern** is constructor over-injection — when a class takes more than 5-7 constructor parameters, it signals a **Single Responsibility Principle violation** rather than an injection style problem.

Refactoring over-injected constructors involves identifying cohesive groups of dependencies and extracting them into focused services. An `OrderService` with eight dependencies almost certainly handles too many concerns — billing, inventory, notifications, and auditing all crammed into one class.

```java
// BEFORE — 8 dependencies indicate SRP violation
public OrderService(OrderRepo repo, PaymentService pay,
    InventoryService inv, NotificationService notify,
    AuditService audit, PricingService price,
    TaxService tax, ShippingService ship) { ... }

// AFTER — extracted focused collaborators
public OrderService(OrderRepo repo,
    OrderFulfillment fulfillment,  // wraps inventory + shipping
    OrderBilling billing,          // wraps payment + pricing + tax
    OrderNotifier notifier) { ... }
```

The refactored version has four parameters, each representing a cohesive responsibility. The original eight are still injected — they just live in the right classes now.

**Circular dependency detection** differs by injection style. Constructor injection fails fast at startup with `BeanCurrentlyInCreationException`, which is a feature — it forces you to fix the design. Field and setter injection hide circular dependencies at startup and may cause subtle runtime bugs when beans are partially initialized.

When you encounter a circular dependency, the fix is usually to **extract the shared concern** into a separate bean, use **domain events** to decouple the interaction, or apply the interface segregation principle so each bean depends on a narrow interface rather than a concrete class. Spring's `@Lazy` on one constructor parameter can break the cycle as a temporary workaround, but it masks a design problem that should be addressed.

The typical pattern is to mark one implementation as **`@Primary`** — the default that most injection points receive — and use `@Qualifier` only at injection points that need a specific alternative. This keeps most of your code clean while giving you precise control where it matters.

Consider an application with two notification channels. The **`EmailNotifier`** handles most cases, but certain services need to send SMS alerts instead. The `@Primary` annotation on `EmailNotifier` means every plain `Notifier` injection point gets email by default, without any qualifier needed.

```java
public interface Notifier {
    void send(String message);
}

@Primary  // default — most injection points get this
@Component
public class EmailNotifier implements Notifier {
    public void send(String message) { /* send email */ }
}

@Component("smsNotifier")
public class SmsNotifier implements Notifier {
    public void send(String message) { /* send SMS */ }
}
```

With these definitions, any service that declares `Notifier notifier` in its constructor gets `EmailNotifier` automatically. Only services that explicitly need SMS use `@Qualifier`.

```java
@Service
public class AlertService {
    // Gets EmailNotifier — @Primary wins when no qualifier is specified
    public AlertService(Notifier notifier) { ... }
}

@Service
public class EmergencyService {
    // Overrides @Primary — explicitly requests the SMS implementation
    public EmergencyService(@Qualifier("smsNotifier") Notifier notifier) { ... }
}
```

The **`@Qualifier` value must match the bean name** — defaulting to the class name with a lowercase first letter, or a custom name in `@Component("name")`. A common mistake is using the original casing — `@Qualifier("SmsNotifier")` instead of `@Qualifier("smsNotifier")` — which produces a confusing `NoSuchBeanDefinitionException` at startup.

Beyond `@Primary` and `@Qualifier`, Spring has one more fallback: **field or parameter name matching**. If you declare `private Notifier smsNotifier`, Spring will match a bean named `smsNotifier` even without `@Qualifier`. This works but creates fragile coupling between variable names and bean registration — rename the field and injection silently breaks.

For applications with many qualified beans, **custom qualifier annotations** are the scalable approach. Instead of scattering string-based `@Qualifier("stripe")` across the codebase — where a typo silently fails — you create a type-safe annotation.

```java
@Qualifier
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Target({ElementType.FIELD, ElementType.PARAMETER, ElementType.TYPE})
public @interface Stripe {}

@Stripe @Component
public class StripeGateway implements PaymentGateway { ... }

@Service
public class CheckoutService {
    // Compile-time safety — typos are caught by the compiler
    public CheckoutService(@Stripe PaymentGateway gateway) { ... }
}
```

The custom annotation is compile-time safe — if you rename it, every usage fails to compile. String-based qualifiers only fail at runtime. This pattern is common in production codebases with multiple database connections, messaging providers, or external API clients.

The `${property.key}` syntax resolves against Spring's `Environment`, which aggregates properties from `application.properties`, environment variables, system properties, and command-line arguments. Spring walks these sources in priority order and returns the first match.

Resolution happens at **bean creation time**, not at method call time. The value is injected once and cached in the field. If the property changes in the file after startup, the bean still holds the old value — there's no live reload unless you use Spring Cloud Config's `@RefreshScope`.

```java
@Service
public class PaymentClient {
    @Value("${payment.api.url}")
    private String apiUrl;

    @Value("${payment.timeout:5000}")
    private int timeoutMs;

    @Value("${payment.retries:3}")
    private int maxRetries;
}
```

The colon acts as a separator between the key and the default value. Watch for a subtle trap: there's **no space after the colon**. Writing `${key: default}` injects `" default"` with a leading space, which causes silent bugs in URLs and numeric parsing. Always use `${key:default}` without spaces.

While `${}` performs simple property lookup, `#{}` evaluates a **Spring Expression Language** expression. SpEL can call methods, access system properties, reference other beans, and perform arithmetic — which makes it powerful but also harder to debug when something goes wrong.

```java
@Value("#{systemProperties['java.version']}")
private String javaVersion;

@Value("#{T(Runtime).getRuntime().availableProcessors()}")
private int cpuCores;

@Value("#{'${allowed.origins}'.split(',')}")
private List<String> allowedOrigins;
```

The combined syntax in the third example — `#{'${key}'.split(',')}` — first resolves the property placeholder, then applies SpEL to the result. This is the standard pattern for converting a comma-separated property into a `List`. Note the nesting: `${}` is inside `#{}`, never the reverse.

SpEL in `@Value` is evaluated at startup. If the expression fails — a null property, a class not found, a method that doesn't exist — you get a `SpelEvaluationException` and the application won't start. Keep expressions simple. If you need complex logic, move it to a `@PostConstruct` method where you can handle errors gracefully and log meaningful diagnostics.

The moment you have three or more `@Value` fields sharing a prefix — `${mail.host}`, `${mail.port}`, `${mail.username}` — you should migrate to `@ConfigurationProperties`. The benefits compound as the configuration group grows.

`@ConfigurationProperties` binds an entire prefix to a POJO. Fields map to properties automatically. Nested objects, lists, and maps work without SpEL. Most importantly, you can add `@Validated` and use **JSR-303 annotations** — `@NotBlank`, `@Min(1)`, `@Max(65535)` — to validate at startup. A missing required property fails with a clear message listing exactly what's wrong, instead of a generic `IllegalArgumentException`.

```java
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "mail")
@Validated
public class MailProperties {
    @NotBlank private String host;
    @Min(1) @Max(65535) private int port = 587;
    private Duration connectionTimeout = Duration.ofSeconds(30);
}
```

Spring parses `Duration` values like `20s`, `5m`, or `PT30S` automatically — no conversion code needed. Spring Boot's annotation processor generates `spring-configuration-metadata.json` from `@ConfigurationProperties` classes, giving IDEs auto-completion for your custom properties. Your team stops guessing property names — the IDE tells them. This alone justifies the migration for any property group used by more than one developer.
